<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Conimbriga</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Conimbriga is the site of a Roman town near Condeixa-a-Nova, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> in central Portugal. A pre-Roman settlement that flourished under the empire, it gained baths, a forum and houses with fine mosaics, and in the late Roman period was enclosed by a new wall that cut through the older town. It was sacked by the Suebi in the 460s, and its bishopric later moved to Aeminium, which took the name Coimbra.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/conimbriga.jpg" alt="Conimbriga" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conimbriga_(13249222603).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
